        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0027/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0027</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0027/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Borel modality">Borel modality</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">growth</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="area">height</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>For an algebraic group action, modality is the maximum number of
parameters of orbits. Write <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {mod}(B : \mathfrak {n}_{n})]]></fr:tex> for the modality of
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B = T \ltimes  U_{n}]]></fr:tex> acting on its nilradical
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathfrak {n}_{n}]]></fr:tex>, and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {mod}(Q : V)]]></fr:tex> for the analogous invariant of a
parabolic or reductive factor <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Q]]></fr:tex> acting on a module <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p>This is the one invariant both halves of the program share: it bounds
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\deg  \kappa (U_{n})]]></fr:tex> from above through <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0029/" title="Theorem D: the modality cap" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0029/" display-uri="ffi-0029" type="local">Theorem D</fr:link>, and it is what the
height ladder spends — <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {mod}(B : \mathfrak {n}_{n}) = 1]]></fr:tex> is exactly why
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0020/" title="Height at n ≤ 7" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0020/" display-uri="ffi-0020" type="local">height at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  7]]></fr:tex></fr:link> goes through and why <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 8]]></fr:tex> does
not.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: journal sec:modality; published rows Goddard--Goodwin, GMR
2014.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0038/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0038</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0038/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The degree law">The degree law</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>conjecture</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">growth</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">open</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p><fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \deg  \kappa (U_{n}) = \lfloor  n(n+6)/12 \rfloor ,
]]></fr:tex>
with equality in the modality cap: <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\deg  \kappa (U_{n}) = (n-1) + \operatorname {mod}(B : \mathfrak {n}_{n})]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Status.</html:strong> The lower bound is a theorem, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0037/" title="The growth theorem" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0037/" display-uri="ffi-0037" type="local">the growth theorem</fr:link>;
the upper bound is <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0029/" title="Theorem D: the modality cap" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0029/" display-uri="ffi-0029" type="local">Theorem D</fr:link>; what is conjectural is that they
meet. Both sides are known for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  9]]></fr:tex>. For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 10 \dots  16]]></fr:tex> the
resulting lower bounds on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {mod}(B : \mathfrak {n}_{n})]]></fr:tex> — from 4 up to 14 — are
unconditional, using VLA 2003, GMR 2014 and the Pak--Soffer Appendix A
degrees. Verified <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  16]]></fr:tex>; the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 17]]></fr:tex> prediction (degree 32) runs
ahead of the literature.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0026/" title="The class count kappa(U_n)" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0026/" display-uri="ffi-0026" type="local">The class count kappa(U_n)</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0029/" title="Theorem D: the modality cap" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0029/" display-uri="ffi-0029" type="local">Theorem D: the modality cap</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0037/" title="The growth theorem" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0037/" display-uri="ffi-0037" type="local">The growth theorem</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: lc_law.md Theorem C/D; scratch_nineteenth.md for the
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  17]]></fr:tex> table.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0037/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0037</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0037/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The growth theorem">The growth theorem</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">growth</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>For every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex> and every prime power <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \kappa (U_{n}(\mathbb {F}_q)) \ge  q^{\lfloor  n(n+6)/12 \rfloor } \bigl (1 + O(q^{-1/2})\bigr )
]]></fr:tex>
along Frobenius towers in every characteristic. Consequently, where
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa (U_{n})]]></fr:tex> is polynomial, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\deg  \kappa (U_{n}) \ge  \lfloor  n(n+6)/12 \rfloor ]]></fr:tex>; and combined
with <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0029/" title="Theorem D: the modality cap" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0029/" display-uri="ffi-0029" type="local">Theorem D</fr:link>, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \operatorname {mod}(B : \mathfrak {n}_{n}) \ge  \lfloor  n(n+6)/12 \rfloor  - (n-1)
]]></fr:tex>
for every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>, unconditionally.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Mechanism.</html:strong> Rosenlicht plus Lang on the sweep strata. The generic strata
of maximal <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex>-modality each contribute
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q^{\operatorname {umod}}(1 + O(q^{-1/2}))]]></fr:tex> Frobenius-stable orbits, and
disjointness makes the contributions add. The floor is attained by the
explicit period-6 maximizer shapes of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0034/" title="(P4): the maximum of umod over compositions" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0034/" display-uri="ffi-0034" type="local">(P4)</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Warning.</html:strong> This re-proves Higman's <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q^{\lfloor  n(n+6)/12 \rfloor }]]></fr:tex> lower bound
(Vera-López--Arregi 2003 for small <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>). Check
h2-literature-attribution before claiming new content here.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0026/" title="The class count kappa(U_n)" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0026/" display-uri="ffi-0026" type="local">The class count kappa(U_n)</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0029/" title="Theorem D: the modality cap" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0029/" display-uri="ffi-0029" type="local">Theorem D: the modality cap</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0033/" title="(Deg-Opt): the grand formula for umod" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0033/" display-uri="ffi-0033" type="local">(Deg-Opt): the grand formula for umod</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0034/" title="(P4): the maximum of umod over compositions" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0034/" display-uri="ffi-0034" type="local">(P4): the maximum of umod over compositions</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: lc_law.md Theorem C, Corollary H6.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/sec-growth/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>sec-growth</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/sec-growth/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="finite-field-invariants: a reference forest › The growth program"><fr:link href="/ffi/index/" title="finite-field-invariants: a reference forest" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/index/" display-uri="index" type="local">finite-field-invariants: a reference forest</fr:link> › The growth program</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Higman's class count, reached through the same Borel-orbit
  geometry. The chain from the definition of modality to the leading
  term of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa (U_{n})]]></fr:tex> is complete except for one inequality, and the
  degree half of it is a theorem for every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:ul><html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0026/" title="The class count kappa(U_n)" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0026/" display-uri="ffi-0026" type="local">The class count</fr:link> — Higman's program.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0027/" title="Borel modality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0027/" display-uri="ffi-0027" type="local">Borel modality</fr:link> — modality of the Borel on its
    nilradical.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0028/" title="U-modality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0028/" display-uri="ffi-0028" type="local"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex>-modality</fr:link> — modality of the unipotent
    group.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0029/" title="Theorem D: the modality cap" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0029/" display-uri="ffi-0029" type="local">Theorem D</fr:link> — the bridge to the class count.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0030/" title="z_u(c) and the modality formula" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0030/" display-uri="ffi-0030" type="local"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[z_u(c)]]></fr:tex></fr:link> — the generic centralizer dimension.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0031/" title="The thread algebra" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0031/" display-uri="ffi-0031" type="local">The thread algebra</fr:link> — the algebra that computes it.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0032/" title="The graded count for z_u (H4)" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0032/" display-uri="ffi-0032" type="local">The graded count</fr:link> — the graded count and the defect.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0033/" title="(Deg-Opt): the grand formula for umod" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0033/" display-uri="ffi-0033" type="local">(Deg-Opt)</fr:link> — the closed form.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0034/" title="(P4): the maximum of umod over compositions" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0034/" display-uri="ffi-0034" type="local">(P4)</fr:link> — the maximum, and where it is attained.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0035/" title="(P5): the grand formula off the unimodal locus" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0035/" display-uri="ffi-0035" type="local">(P5)</fr:link> — the off-unimodal extension.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0036/" title="(P6): the collapse identity and the period-6 structure" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0036/" display-uri="ffi-0036" type="local">(P6)</fr:link> — the collapse identity and the origin of
    period 6.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0037/" title="The growth theorem" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0037/" display-uri="ffi-0037" type="local">The growth theorem</fr:link> — the unconditional lower bound.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0038/" title="The degree law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0038/" display-uri="ffi-0038" type="local">The degree law</fr:link> — the degree.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0039/" title="The leading-term law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0039/" display-uri="ffi-0039" type="local">The leading-term law</fr:link> — the leading coefficient.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0040/" title="The M-law: the unimodal side of the leading-term law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0040/" display-uri="ffi-0040" type="local">The M-law</fr:link> — its proven half.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0041/" title="(TOP-X): rigidity of maximal modality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0041/" display-uri="ffi-0041" type="local">(TOP-X)</fr:link> — the rigidity statement it needs.</html:li></html:ul>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
