            <fr:title text="The class count kappa(U_n)">The class count kappa(U_n)</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">growth</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>For the unitriangular group <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U_{n} = U_{n}(\mathbb {F}_q)]]></fr:tex>, let
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa (U_{n}) = \kappa (U_{n}(\mathbb {F}_q))]]></fr:tex> denote its number of conjugacy classes.
<html:strong>Higman's conjecture</html:strong>: <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa (U_{n})]]></fr:tex> is polynomial in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex> for each
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p>Where polynomiality is known — <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  16]]></fr:tex>, Pak--Soffer — write
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\deg  \kappa (U_{n})]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {lc} \kappa (U_{n})]]></fr:tex> for the degree and the leading coefficient.
The growth program is the study of these two numbers:
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0038/" title="The degree law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0038/" display-uri="ffi-0038" type="local">the degree law</fr:link> for the degree,
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0039/" title="The leading-term law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0039/" display-uri="ffi-0039" type="local">the leading-term law</fr:link> for the leading coefficient.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: Higman; Pak--Soffer App.~A for the known values.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0038/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0038</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0038/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The degree law">The degree law</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>conjecture</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">growth</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">open</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p><fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \deg  \kappa (U_{n}) = \lfloor  n(n+6)/12 \rfloor ,
]]></fr:tex>
with equality in the modality cap: <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\deg  \kappa (U_{n}) = (n-1) + \operatorname {mod}(B : \mathfrak {n}_{n})]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Status.</html:strong> The lower bound is a theorem, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0037/" title="The growth theorem" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0037/" display-uri="ffi-0037" type="local">the growth theorem</fr:link>;
the upper bound is <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0029/" title="Theorem D: the modality cap" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0029/" display-uri="ffi-0029" type="local">Theorem D</fr:link>; what is conjectural is that they
meet. Both sides are known for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  9]]></fr:tex>. For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 10 \dots  16]]></fr:tex> the
resulting lower bounds on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {mod}(B : \mathfrak {n}_{n})]]></fr:tex> — from 4 up to 14 — are
unconditional, using VLA 2003, GMR 2014 and the Pak--Soffer Appendix A
degrees. Verified <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  16]]></fr:tex>; the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 17]]></fr:tex> prediction (degree 32) runs
ahead of the literature.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0026/" title="The class count kappa(U_n)" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0026/" display-uri="ffi-0026" type="local">The class count kappa(U_n)</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0029/" title="Theorem D: the modality cap" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0029/" display-uri="ffi-0029" type="local">Theorem D: the modality cap</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0037/" title="The growth theorem" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0037/" display-uri="ffi-0037" type="local">The growth theorem</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: lc_law.md Theorem C/D; scratch_nineteenth.md for the
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  17]]></fr:tex> table.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0037/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0037</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0037/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The growth theorem">The growth theorem</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">growth</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>For every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex> and every prime power <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \kappa (U_{n}(\mathbb {F}_q)) \ge  q^{\lfloor  n(n+6)/12 \rfloor } \bigl (1 + O(q^{-1/2})\bigr )
]]></fr:tex>
along Frobenius towers in every characteristic. Consequently, where
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa (U_{n})]]></fr:tex> is polynomial, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\deg  \kappa (U_{n}) \ge  \lfloor  n(n+6)/12 \rfloor ]]></fr:tex>; and combined
with <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0029/" title="Theorem D: the modality cap" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0029/" display-uri="ffi-0029" type="local">Theorem D</fr:link>, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \operatorname {mod}(B : \mathfrak {n}_{n}) \ge  \lfloor  n(n+6)/12 \rfloor  - (n-1)
]]></fr:tex>
for every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>, unconditionally.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Mechanism.</html:strong> Rosenlicht plus Lang on the sweep strata. The generic strata
of maximal <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex>-modality each contribute
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q^{\operatorname {umod}}(1 + O(q^{-1/2}))]]></fr:tex> Frobenius-stable orbits, and
disjointness makes the contributions add. The floor is attained by the
explicit period-6 maximizer shapes of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0034/" title="(P4): the maximum of umod over compositions" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0034/" display-uri="ffi-0034" type="local">(P4)</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Warning.</html:strong> This re-proves Higman's <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q^{\lfloor  n(n+6)/12 \rfloor }]]></fr:tex> lower bound
(Vera-López--Arregi 2003 for small <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>). Check
h2-literature-attribution before claiming new content here.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0026/" title="The class count kappa(U_n)" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0026/" display-uri="ffi-0026" type="local">The class count kappa(U_n)</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0029/" title="Theorem D: the modality cap" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0029/" display-uri="ffi-0029" type="local">Theorem D: the modality cap</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0033/" title="(Deg-Opt): the grand formula for umod" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0033/" display-uri="ffi-0033" type="local">(Deg-Opt): the grand formula for umod</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0034/" title="(P4): the maximum of umod over compositions" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0034/" display-uri="ffi-0034" type="local">(P4): the maximum of umod over compositions</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: lc_law.md Theorem C, Corollary H6.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0009</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0009/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The height conjecture">The height conjecture</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>conjecture</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="area">height</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">open</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>For every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex> and every prime power <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \operatorname {ht} J = \frac {n(n+1)}{2},
  \qquad  \text {equivalently} \qquad 
  \dim  V(J) = \frac {n(n - 1)}{2}:
]]></fr:tex>
the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\frac {n(n+1)}{2}]]></fr:tex> invariants <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}^{i}(c_{j})]]></fr:tex>,
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0 \le  i < j \le  n]]></fr:tex>, form a regular sequence in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> and extend to a
homogeneous system of parameters. The conjectured dimension is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  \mathrm {Fl}]]></fr:tex> — up to dimension, the
flag witnesses must be everything.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Status.</html:strong> Proven for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  5]]></fr:tex> every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>
(<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0019/" title="Height at n ≤ 5" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0019/" display-uri="ffi-0019" type="local">height at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  5]]></fr:tex></fr:link>); for characteristic <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0]]></fr:tex>, every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>;
for all but finitely many characteristics per <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>, ineffectively; and
for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  9]]></fr:tex> every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex> (<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0018/" title="Height at n ≤ 9" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0018/" display-uri="ffi-0018" type="local">height at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  9]]></fr:tex></fr:link>). Open in
general, and what is open is exactly <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0005/" title="The Steenrod ideal" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/" display-uri="ffi-0005" type="local">The Steenrod ideal</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0008/" title="The Krull bound: only the upper bound is open" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0008/" display-uri="ffi-0008" type="local">The Krull bound: only the upper bound is open</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: distilled.tex Part 1; journal thm:height-n7, thm:m8,
cor:height-n8, cor:height-n9.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0028/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0028</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0028/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="U-modality">U-modality</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">growth</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>For a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex>-stable irreducible subvariety <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W \subseteq  \mathfrak {n}]]></fr:tex>,
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \operatorname {umod}(W) = \dim  W - \max _{y \in  W} \dim  U \cdot  y,
]]></fr:tex>
the dimension of the generic-orbit space; and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {umod}(w) =
\operatorname {umod}(\overline {\text {genStr}(w)})]]></fr:tex> per sweep stratum.</html:p>
            <html:p>For a composition <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c \vdash  n]]></fr:tex> with block sizes <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_i]]></fr:tex>, write
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {umod}(\mathfrak {n}_{c})]]></fr:tex> for the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex>-modality of the parabolic
nilradical slice. These parabolic values are the ones with a closed
form: <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0033/" title="(Deg-Opt): the grand formula for umod" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0033/" display-uri="ffi-0033" type="local">(Deg-Opt)</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0027/" title="Borel modality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0027/" display-uri="ffi-0027" type="local">Borel modality</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: journal sec:modality.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
